# Games, played far enough in to show a board rather than a
# title screen.

shot    advint
size    36 80
for     advint advcom
cap     ADVSYS -- David Betz's adventure system -- compiled and then played,
cap     both on this disk.  advcom builds a world from GAMES/ADVSYS/
cap     osample.adv and advint runs it.  This card used to say that needed
cap     a shell with a real chd, which bash has not got.  It does not: the
cap     two limits are that advcom opens its `@objects.adi' include by bare
cap     name in the DATA DIRECTORY, and that it holds a filename in 20
cap     characters and appends `.adv' to it.  Put both files where the data
cap     directory already is and give it a short name, and bash compiles it
cap     -- `advcom osample', not `advcom /dd/GAMES/ADVSYS/osample'.
run     cat /dd/GAMES/ADVSYS/osample.adv > /dd/osample.adv
wait    8
run     cat /dd/GAMES/ADVSYS/objects.adi > /dd/objects.adi
wait    8
run     advcom osample > /nil
wait    35
run     advint osample
wait    12
send    look\r
wait    6
send    n\r
wait    6
send    look\r
wait    8

shot    bincheckr
cap     bincheckr reads GNU Chess's opening book -- and reports booksize 0
cap     for a book of 145 KB, then aborts.  DOC/STATUS credited it as
cap     working given a file; this is what it actually does.
run     bincheckr /dd/GAMES/gnuchess.book
wait    8
kill

shot    blackjak
cap     Las Vegas blackjack, SNOBOL4 again, with a hand dealt and a bet on
cap     the table.
run     blackjak
wait    5
send    no\r
wait    5
send    10\r
wait    8

shot    checkgame
cap     `checkgame' and `game' are meant to replay a saved chess game move
cap     by move.  Given the opening book that ships here, this is what they
cap     do: abort, with no output at all.  DOC/STATUS has them among the
cap     eighteen crashes.
run     checkgame /dd/GAMES/gnuchess.book 1 3
wait    8

shot    convert
for     convert vtxtcn mkindex advcom
cap     The data builders: `convert' and `vtxtcn' make WORLD's tables, and
cap     advcom compiles an ADVSYS world.  They write files and print
cap     little, which is why the sweep could never credit them.
run     advcom
wait    8
run     convert
wait    8
run     mkindex
wait    8
run     vtxtcn
wait    10

shot    fuddle
cap     fuddle, a chess variant, one move in.
run     fuddle
wait    6
send    y\r
wait    6
send    e2e4\r
wait    10

shot    gnuchess
for     gnuchess gnuchessn
cap     GNU Chess, after 1. e4 e5.  It needs `. /dd/SYS/termcap.entry' first
cap     -- its termcap library reads TERMCAP as the capability string itself
cap     rather than as the name of a file, so the plain TERMCAP=/dd/SYS/termcap
cap     that SYS/login sets gets it `'vt100': Unknown terminal type'.  With the
cap     entry sourced it draws the board, keeps both clocks, and plays.
run     . /dd/SYS/termcap.entry
wait    4
run     gnuchess
wait    12
send    1\r
wait    12
send    e2e4\r
wait    22
kill

shot    gnuchess-builds
for     gnuchessr infocom.tcap
cap     Two more GNU Chess builds -- ncurses and raw -- kept because they
cap     do not behave the same on every terminal.
run     gnuchessr
wait    8
send    e2e4\r
wait    10

shot    gnuchessc
cap     GNU Chess 4.0 with the curses display, after 1. e4.
run     gnuchessc
wait    6
send    e2e4\r
wait    10

shot    crib
cap     Cribbage, dealt and pegging -- the card fan on the left, the two
cap     scoring tracks on the right.  It needs TERM, so it is run the way a
cap     person meets it, from a login session; bare it says `Unknown
cap     terminal type '''.  `cribbage' beside it is a different program.
run     crib
wait    6
send    n\r
wait    4
send    S\r
wait    4
send    \r
wait    8

shot    hotel
cap     hotel, a two-player board game played by coordinates.  It asks how
cap     many are playing and then draws the board.
run     hotel
wait    5
send    N\r
wait    4
send    2\r
wait    5
send    Alice\r
wait    4
send    Bob\r
wait    8

shot    infocom
for     infocom infocom.tcap
cap     The Infocom Z-machine interpreter playing a real story file --
cap     GAMES/INFORM/dejavu.z3, compiled with Inform and shipped here.
cap     This is the TERMCAP build, `infocom.tcap', which is the one to use:
cap     it puts a proper status line across the top, room name and score.
cap     Plain `infocom' plays the same story and fills the screen with
cap     brackets trying to draw that line.
run     infocom.tcap /dd/GAMES/INFORM/dejavu.z3
wait    8
send    \r
wait    4
send    look\r
wait    6
send    \r
wait    4
send    inventory\r
wait    6

shot    mkdict
for     mkdict mkindex
cap     mkdict builds Boggle's dictionary -- and it TAKES A BUS ERROR on the
cap     word list that ships with the game, after writing 924 bytes of the
cap     80 KB it was given.  The built dictionary is already in GAMES/BOG,
cap     so nothing is lost by not running it; this is what running it does.
run     head -n 400 /dd/GAMES/BOG/dict > /dd/tmp/words
wait    8
run     mkdict < /dd/tmp/words > /dd/tmp/newdict
wait    10
run     wc /dd/tmp/words /dd/tmp/newdict
wait    8

shot    nchess
cap     GNU Chess 4.0 with its plain display -- the board as characters,
cap     which is how it was played over a serial line.
run     nchess
wait    6
send    e2e4\r
wait    8

shot    poker
cap     Cold-hand poker, one of four SNOBOL4 games here -- the interpreter
cap     was compiled to C and its games came with it.  It wants the word
cap     `no', not `N', and says so in its own voice if you try.
run     poker
wait    5
send    no\r
wait    5
send    50\r
wait    6
send    10\r
wait    8

shot    postprint
cap     postprint writes a chess position as PostScript for printing.  It
cap     gets one line out -- the page setup -- and stops there.
run     postprint | head -n 20
wait    8

shot    queens
cap     The N-queens problem, solved and drawn -- an IOCCC entry that reads
cap     the board size on standard input.  Six here, and it prints every
cap     solution it finds.
run     echo 6 | queens | head -n 21
wait    8

shot    stone
cap     The stones game: take stones from the pile, do not take the last
cap     one.  Twenty-one stones, the parity chosen, and the first move
cap     made.  It answers a wrong answer in its own voice.
run     stone
wait    5
send    no\r
wait    5
send    21\r
wait    6
send    1\r
wait    6
send    3\r
wait    8

shot    tttt
cap     Tic-tac-toe on a four-by-four board, mid-game.
run     tttt
wait    4
send    b1\r
wait    3
send    c2\r
wait    4

shot    draw
for     draw
cap     draw, Nils McCarthy's character-graphics drawing program: it rules a
cap     canvas across the terminal and you move a cursor over it laying down
cap     characters.  This is what `?' shows -- vi's hjkl to move, yubn for
cap     the diagonals, `p' to lift and drop the pen, backslash to choose the
cap     character it draws with, and a shell escape.  The keys can be
cap     rebound through a DRAW environment variable.
run     draw
wait    8
keys    ?
wait    6
kill

shot    lorenz3d
for     lorenz3d
cap     The Lorenz attractor in three dimensions, asked for parameter by
cap     parameter: the time step, the z magnification and offset, then
cap     Lorenz's own a, b and c -- 10, 28 and 8/3 here, the values that
cap     make the butterfly.  What it draws with is TEKTRONIX vector codes,
cap     so on a vt100 the picture arrives as characters; the dialogue is
cap     the part worth photographing.
run     lorenz3d
wait    4
send    0.01\r
wait    2
send    20\r
wait    2
send    0\r
wait    2
send    10\r
wait    2
send    28\r
wait    2
send    2.667\r
wait    2
kill

shot    gwindows-games
for     cyberwar pacman puzzle
cap     Three that draw through G-Windows, the graphics system this disk
cap     does not carry.  `cyberwar' and `pacman' write nothing at all and
cap     exit; `puzzle' gets as far as one rule of plus signs -- the top
cap     edge of the tile frame -- and stops there.  They are here because
cap     they are part of what the archives held, not because they can be
cap     played on a terminal.
run     cyberwar
wait    6
run     pacman
wait    6
run     puzzle
wait    6

shot    game
for     game
cap     `game' replays a saved chess game.  Asked with no arguments it
cap     says what it takes; given the opening book that ships here it
cap     aborts, which is the `checkgame' card beside this one.
run     game
wait    8

